{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"custom exception custom exception","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md","fileName":"Custom Exception.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Custom Exception","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-26 22:56\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[C Sharp Exception Handling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Exception Handling","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"custom exception custom exception","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md","fileName":"Custom Exception.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Custom Exception","heading":"Custom Exception","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A custom exception is an application-defined type derived from `Exception` that names a failure not adequately represented by a built-in type. It can provide constructors for a message and an inner exception so callers retain both domain meaning and the original cause.\n\nCreating a custom type is most useful when consumers need to catch that failure distinctly or when the name clarifies a stable application contract. It should not merely rename a standard exception without adding meaning.","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Exception Handling","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Custom Exception.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"custom exception custom exception","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cu